efing for the industrial economy=0A=0A=0AThe Operations=0ABrief=0A=0ABy Bil=
l Murphy, powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.com/]=0A=0A=C2=
=A0=0A=0AOctober 9, 2026=0A=0AIn a budget meeting, a safety repair gets fun=
ded because it has a clear=0Adeadline: fix this or stop production. Another=
 line is still running but loses=0Agood units every week, so its improvemen=
t request sounds easier to defer. The=0Asecond project might save more capa=
city, but you cannot compare them until the=0Aongoing loss is visible.=0A=
=0AThis issue is about that gap: how to show the production loss already ha=
ppening,=0Aestimate what another quarter could cost, and propose a small te=
st to confirm=0Athe fix works before you ask for the full rollout.=0A=0AThi=
s week=E2=80=99s question=0A=0AWhat most often stalls an improvement projec=
t at your operation=3F=0A=0AThe budget goes to urgent work=0A[https://tally=
.so/r/PdMD5d=3Fanswer=3DThe%20budget%20goes%20to%20urgent%20work&email=3D$=
{email}&campaign=3Dtob_poll_2&v=3D1]=0A=0AHard to prove the payoff=0A[https=
://tally.so/r/PdMD5d=3Fanswer=3DHard%20to%20prove%20the%20payoff&email=3D${=
email}&campaign=3Dtob_poll_2&v=3D1]=0A=0ANo one free to run it=0A[https://t=
ally.so/r/PdMD5d=3Fanswer=3DNo%20one%20free%20to%20run%20it&email=3D${email=
}&campaign=3Dtob_poll_2&v=3D1]=0A=0AOne click is enough. I=E2=80=99ll share=
 what we hear in a future issue.=0A=0ABill=0A=0A01The Shift=0A=0A=0AShow th=
e Loss, Not Just the Promised Return=0A=0AA projected return on investment =
can be easy to defer when a repair has a clear=0Adeadline. Make the request=
 comparable by showing the current production loss in=0Ayour own terms.=0A=
=0AA safety repair gets urgency because the consequence is immediate. An=0A=
improvement project sounds optional when you frame it as future capacity. B=
ut if=0Ayour records show that a line loses good units every week, or runs =
in downtime=0Athat could be eliminated, that loss is happening now. The que=
stion is whether to=0Aact on it this quarter or carry it forward.=0A=0AShow=
 the loss in your operation=E2=80=99s own terms: downtime hours, good units=
 scrapped=0Aor off-specification, or capacity hours displaced by rework. Th=
en calculate what=0Awaiting could mean. Over the next comparable period, if=
 the same pattern=0Arepeats, what production could be at risk=3F This does =
not guarantee approval; it=0Amakes the improvement request comparable to an=
 urgent repair.=0A=0ABrey-Krause lost weeks on its mirror line after equipm=
ent failure=0A[https://www.nist.gov/mep/successstories/2024/mrc-process-imp=
rovement-roadmap-delivers-capacity-and-sales-increases-while].=0AIts MEP pa=
rtner recommended replacing the straightener and notcher first, with=0Alate=
r upgrades on a roadmap. NIST reports 15% less downtime and 15% capacity=0A=
growth for that company. Those are site-specific reported results, not a=0A=
forecast for your operation or proof of a general budget rule.=0A=0AShow wh=
at the loss actually costs before you forecast what a fix would save.=0A=0A=
02From the Floor=0A=0A=0AWhy the Improvement Waits=0A=0AFrom the second Ope=
rations Brief Roundtable: why high-value work keeps losing to=0Aurgent work=
.=0A=0AMadhur Bedre [https://www.linkedin.com/in/madhur-bedre/]=E2=80=99s =
process-optimization=0Aprojects compete with urgent maintenance and safety =
work for plant budget. He=0Adescribed what that fight looks like from his s=
ide of the table:=0A=0A=E2=80=9CWhat we work on would be high value, but it=
=E2=80=99s not necessarily urgent=E2=80=A6 You=E2=80=99re=0Afighting for th=
e same dollar, and maintenance definitely gets the priority over=0Aus.=E2=
=80=9D=0A=0AMadhur Bedre [https://www.linkedin.com/in/madhur-bedre/]=0A=0AP=
resident, Atlas Prediction Control [https://atlasprx.com/]. The Operations=
=0ABrief Roundtable No. 02, October 2026=0A[https://www.theoperationsbrief.=
com/roundtable/rt02-worth-the-investment/]=0A=0AHis answer is to translate =
the work into what the plant already tracks, such as=0Aproduction rate, fla=
ring and off-quality material, and put an ROI on it. That=0Ashows what doin=
g nothing already costs, not just the upside you hope to gain=0Alater.=0A=
=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AAn improvement framed as future upside will keep losin=
g to urgent work. Framed=0Aas a loss you can already measure, it gets a fai=
r hearing. The Stack below shows=0Ahow to put that loss on one page.=0A=0AR=
ead the full Roundtable No. 02 recap =E2=86=92=0A[https://www.theoperations=
brief.com/roundtable/rt02-worth-the-investment/]=0A=0A03The Stack=0A=0A=0AB=
uild a One-Page Case for One Deferred Fix=0A=0AWork with an operations lead=
 and a finance partner. Pull records from shift,=0Amaintenance, quality, an=
d production to build a decision card that fits beside=0Aurgent requests at=
 the next budget meeting.=0A=0ASample decision card (illustrative numbers, =
not a reported plant result)=0A=0ADecision=0ATest a changeover-jam fix on o=
ne packaging line before a larger rollout.=0A=0AOwner=0AProduction supervis=
or; finance partner checks the cost assumptions.=0A=0APresent loss=0AOver 1=
2 weeks, records show 30 stop hours. A supervisor verifies that 12 hours=0A=
came from the jam this fix addresses; exclude the other 18.=0A=0ACapacity a=
t risk=0AAt 40 good units per staffed hour, 12 =C3=97 40 =3D up to 480 good=
 units of capacity,=0Anot 480 guaranteed sales.=0A=0ACost of waiting=0AIf t=
he same verified pattern repeats over the next comparable 12 weeks, up to=
=0Aanother 480 units of capacity could be at risk. Finance checks actual de=
mand,=0Acontribution, scrap or overtime overlap, and fix cost before assign=
ing dollars.=0ADo not count the same loss twice.=0A=0AFirst phase=0ARun a f=
our-week test on that line. Agree in advance on the improvement threshold=
=0Aand the stop-or-expand decision. If the records show little fixable loss=
, or the=0Atest does not reduce it, do not make the larger claim.=0A=0AOpti=
onal AI step=0AIf cause notes are messy, use AI only to suggest which notes=
 mention the jam. A=0Aperson checks every label against the original record=
s. Skip this when cause=0Acodes are already clean; the totals and math come=
 from plant records.=0A=0AThis is a hypothetical card, not an observed cust=
omer result. It is our own=0Aapplication of the panel=E2=80=99s discussion,=
 not a template from any panelist.=0A=0ABring one such page to the next bud=
get discussion. It informs a decision without=0Aoverruling a safety obligat=
ion. Show the cost of doing nothing, propose a test=0Ato confirm the fix wo=
rks, and let the numbers speak for the improvement.=0A=0AThe Operations Bri=
ef=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy, powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.co=
m/]=0A=0AA weekly briefing on the decisions, people, and systems shaping ma=
nufacturing=0Aand distribution.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AColony Spark, 170 Mason St=
reet, Greenwich, Connecticut 06830, United States=0A=0AYou=E2=80=99re recei=
ving this because you subscribed to The Operations Brief.=0A=0A=C2=A9 2026 =
